\subtree{
  \title{Still wrapping up the semester...}

  \p{Last week I wrote "There is still plenty of work to do... but the pace will be much more relaxed."  I always forget that this is completely false.  Last week was \em{not} more relaxed, it was a zoo.  I can only assume this coming week will be the same (in particular, my department is trying to hire a 1-year visitor at the last minute, and I am the chair of the department and hence the chair of the hiring committee); but there is light at the end of the tunnel.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Pride goeth before the fall}

  \p{In last week's piano corner, I said "Through an unlikely series of events, it looks like I may have an opportunity to acquire a free... piano".  I am extremely ashamed to have to write this, but it turns out to have been a scam. (The "piano" was free, but of course the shipping was not...)  Fortunately, I did not end up giving the scammers any money—but only very narrowly.  I actually got as far as attempting to send them money via Venmo, but Venmo stopped the payment because it suspected a scam. This, coupled with a friend's skepticism, finally got me to dig more deeply and uncover incontrovertible proof that the whole thing was a scam all along.}

  \p{This has been a very humbling and sobering experience.  I like to think of myself as sophisticated, educated, intelligent—just as, I assume, many of us do—and therefore immune to scams or misinformation.  But that seems to have actually made it worse.  I wanted so badly for it to be true that I used my sophisticated, educated intelligence to spin all sorts of justifications for why it was not actually a scam despite the (many!) red flags.}

  \p{In the end, I am really thankful that I only lost some pride, not money, and I hope that this experience will help me be more careful and less trusting of my own judgment in the future.}
}

\transclude{00GN}
